Q: cs50 pset5 segmentation fault while locating memory A: <p>In analyzing the above code, I added in some print statements within the dictionary load function to zero in on the possible segmentation fault issue.</p>
<pre><code>bool load(const char *dictionary)
{
    char dicword[LENGTH + 1];
    FILE *dict = fopen(dictionary, &quot;r&quot;);
    if(dict == NULL)
    {
        return false;
    }

    printf(&quot;Loading dictionary %s\n&quot;, dictionary);

    while(fscanf(dict,&quot;%s&quot;, dicword) != EOF)
    {
        node *n = malloc(sizeof(node));
        if(n == NULL)
        {
            return false;
        }

        printf(&quot;Node address: %p  hash: %d\n&quot;, n, hash(dicword));

        strcpy(n -&gt; word, dicword);
        n -&gt; next = table[hash(dicword)];
        table[hash(dicword)] = n;
        words++;
    };
    isLoadedd = true;
    return true;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Adding in a simple main function to load and unload the dictionary, following was the terminal output.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Spell_2/bin/Release$ ./Spell_2 &quot;Stuff.txt&quot;
Loading dictionary Stuff.txt
Node address: 0x562f2ab448a0  word: apple  hash: 847
Node address: 0x562f2ab448e0  word: axis  hash: 855
Node address: 0x562f2ab44920  word: banana  hash: 858
Node address: 0x562f2ab44960  word: beach  hash: 862
Node address: 0x562f2ab449a0  word: forest  hash: 976
Node address: 0x562f2ab449e0  word: ibeam  hash: 1041
Segmentation fault (core dumped)
</code></pre>
<p>The hash values to be used for the table array far exceeds the stated size of &quot;676&quot;, but finally caused the segment fault with the word &quot;ibeam&quot;.  That led to the analysis of the hashing function and a quick look found that the execution of the &quot;toupper&quot; function was misplaced.</p>
<pre><code>unsigned int hash(const char *word)
{
    // TODO: Improve this hash function
    if (strlen(word) == 1)
    {
        return toupper((word[0]) - 'A') * 26;                       /* toupper in the wrong spot */
    }
    return toupper((word[0]) - 'A') * 26 + toupper(word[1]) - 'A';  /* toupper in the wrong spot */
}
</code></pre>
<p>What is happening is that the ASCII value of 'A' (65) is being subtracted from the ASCII value of the first character, in this case 'i', which has a value of &quot;105&quot;.  This nets to an integer value of &quot;40&quot;.  Since this value is not actually an alphabetical character, the &quot;toupper&quot; function effectively does not modify it and so it stays at a value of &quot;40&quot;.  This value is then multiplied by &quot;26&quot; to result in the product &quot;1040&quot;.  Finally, the second portion of the equation adds a value of &quot;1&quot; to this product generating the value of &quot;1041&quot;.</p>
<pre><code>ibeam --&gt; (105 - 65) * 26 + 66 - 65 = 1041 (ASCII code for 'i' is 105)
</code></pre>
<p>Following is the refactored code within the hashing function with the &quot;toupper&quot; function call in the proper spot.</p>
<pre><code>unsigned int hash(const char *word)
{
    // TODO: Improve this hash function
    if (strlen(word) == 1)
    {
        return (toupper(word[0]) - 'A') * 26;
    }
    return (toupper(word[0]) - 'A') * 26 + toupper(word[1]) - 'A';
}
</code></pre>
<p>And following is a retest with the terminal output.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Spell_2/bin/Release$ ./Spell_2 &quot;Stuff.txt&quot;
Loading dictionary Stuff.txt
Node address: 0x561c6e6688a0  word: apple  hash: 15
Node address: 0x561c6e6688e0  word: axis  hash: 23
Node address: 0x561c6e668920  word: banana  hash: 26
Node address: 0x561c6e668960  word: beach  hash: 30
Node address: 0x561c6e6689a0  word: forest  hash: 144
Node address: 0x561c6e6689e0  word: ibeam  hash: 209
Node address: 0x561c6e668a20  word: level  hash: 290
Node address: 0x561c6e668a60  word: men  hash: 316
Node address: 0x561c6e668aa0  word: north  hash: 352
Node address: 0x561c6e668ae0  word: open  hash: 379
Node address: 0x561c6e668b20  word: parting  hash: 390
Node address: 0x561c6e668b60  word: school  hash: 470
Node address: 0x561c6e668ba0  word: tourist  hash: 508
Node address: 0x561c6e668be0  word: zoo  hash: 664
</code></pre>
<p>The simple answer here is that this was a typo in the code; however, the main takeaway here would be to either spend time learning how to use debugging tools or at the least, temporarily add in print statements in various locations in the code to zero in on the cause or causes of the unwanted program behavior.</p>

Q: Property &#39;example&#39; does not exist on type &#39; componentName&#39; A: <p>Several problem:</p>
<p>in interface you have</p>
<pre><code>export interface DataLayer {
    name: {};
    ...
}

</code></pre>
<p>This indicate that name is an Object (well really an Object with no property)</p>
<p>Do you want to say?</p>
<pre><code>export interface DataLayer {
    name: string; //&lt;--if is a string
    ...
}

</code></pre>
<p>In <code>homeComponent</code> you have</p>
<pre><code>dataLayer: DataLayer = {
    name: {{this.results[1][0].name}}
    ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>the use of <code>{{``}}</code> to interpolate is ONLY in template, in code is simply</p>
<pre><code>dataLayer: DataLayer = {
    name: this.results[1][0].name
    ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>The error <code>&quot;Property 'results' does not exist on type 'HomeComponent'.&quot;</code> it's because you are not defined a property 'result' in component.</p>
<p>In HomeComponent define</p>
<pre><code>result:any=null
//or 
result:DataLayer={}

</code></pre>
